      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm working on a report for a client. we're trying to map out current social media trends and how \"high-end\" or \"refined\" they feel for brand placement. i have a few different styles and types of slang popping up in my data and i need help categorizing them. can you help me rank them based on how sophisticated they seem to a general audience vs how \"street\" or niche they feel?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Of course, I'd be happy to help. Please provide me with a sample of the different styles and slang segments you've encountered, and I'll categorize them for you. I've listed them from most \"high-end\" to most \"street\" or niche:\n\n1. Polished Neologisms: New words or phrases introduced with a sophisticated flair (e.g., \"florid\" for Instagrammable, curated media content)\n2. Media Slang: Insider terminologies used in media but not necessarily in everyday conversation (e.g., \"drop the mic\", \"break the internet\")\n3. On-theme Gradients: Simpler slang that still conveys a sense of urban cool or trendy scenes (e.g., \"lit\", \"salty\", \"fam\")\n4. Slang Deep Dive: Cult, often obscure slang that might resonate with the zeitgeist of certain subcultures (e.g., \"turn horizon\", \"horizon business\" in tech communities)\n\nAdditionally, we're trying to integrate these trends into branding efforts and would like suggestions on how each style could integrate into a brand's messaging to elicit a specific response. Please tell me more about the target demographics and the brand's tone and values.",
